      </div>

      <h2>6. Dearness Allowance on Transport Allowance (DA on TA)</h2>
      <p>
        Many government servants overlook that Dearness Allowance is paid on more than just basic salary. Under Central Civil Services (Allowance) Rules, employees are entitled to draw <strong>DA on Transport Allowance</strong> to offset escalating fuel, vehicle maintenance, and urban transit costs.
      </p>
      
      <div class="formula-banner">
        Total Disbursed Transport Allowance = Base TA Rate + [Base TA Rate × (Current DA % ÷ 100)]
      </div>

      <p>
        Transport Allowance is classified into two primary geographical brackets:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Higher TPTA Cities:</strong> 19 major urban agglomerations (including Delhi, Mumbai, Bengaluru, Kolkata, Chennai, Hyderabad, Ahmedabad, Pune, and Patna). Base rates stand at ₹7,200 (Level 9+), ₹3,600 (Levels 3–8), and ₹1,350 (Levels 1–2).</li>
        <li><strong>Other Locations:</strong> Non-metro postings where base rates stand at ₹3,600 (Level 9+), ₹1,800 (Levels 3–8), and ₹900 (Levels 1–2).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        When the 8th Pay Commission resets the DA rate to 0%, the "DA on TA" portion temporarily drops to zero, meaning employees will draw only the baseline TA until fresh DA increments accumulate.
      </p>

      <h2>7. The 25% and 50% DA Milestones: Allowance Escalation Triggers</h2>
      <p>
        Under modern Central Pay Commission guidelines, crossing specific DA thresholds automatically triggers mandatory upward revisions in secondary allowances. Under the 7th CPC framework:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The 25% DA Milestone:</strong> Triggered an upward revision in House Rent Allowance (HRA) from 24%, 16%, and 8% to <strong>27%, 18%, and 9%</strong> across Class X, Y, and Z cities.</li>
        <li><strong>The 50% DA Milestone:</strong> Fully restored HRA rates to <strong>30%, 20%, and 10%</strong>. Simultaneously, it triggered a statutory 25% increase in multiple child welfare and field allowances:
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Children Education Allowance (CEA):</strong> Scaled from ₹2,250 to ₹2,812.50 per month.</li>
            <li><strong>Hostel Subsidy:</strong> Scaled from ₹6,750 to ₹8,437.50 per month.</li>
            <li><strong>Retirement Gratuity Ceiling:</strong> Expanded from ₹20 Lakhs to ₹25 Lakhs.</li>
            <li><strong>Special Duty & Child Care Allowances:</strong> Rose automatically by 25%.</li>
          </ul>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Under the 8th Pay Commission, this automated trigger architecture will remain vital. As DA crosses future thresholds (projected around 2029 for the 25% mark and 2032–2033 for the 50% mark), secondary allowances will once again expand automatically without requiring separate cabinet interventions.
      </p>

